# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files — real secrets never get committed, but the template always is.
# Keep the negation: if the rules above are ever widened to `.env*`, an already-
# tracked .env.example silently stops being tracked in a fresh clone.
.env
.env*.local
!.env.example

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Local Claude Code settings
.claude/settings.local.json

# Client data is TRACKED — deliberate policy, see README -> "Client data policy".
# `clients/`, `intake/`, and `designs/` are version-controlled so client work
# survives outside one machine.
#
# THIS ONLY HOLDS IF THE REMOTE IS PRIVATE. Briefs hold client PII and
# `intake/*.csv` holds PII for *every* form submission, not just the client
# you're building for — and git history is permanent, so a repo that is public
# even briefly cannot be un-leaked by a later commit. Before adding a remote:
#   1. confirm it is private, and
#   2. keep the `deploy-prod` workflow's STRIP_PATHS as the thing that keeps
#      this material off the public `prod` branch.
# Inspiration screenshots are large binaries; they are reference material and
# are not shipped, so keep an eye on repo size.

# Python
__pycache__/
*.pyc
